---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { StructuralGap } from '../../components/islands/StructuralGap'
import { VotedAndNot } from '../../components/islands/VotedAndNot'
import { NetInterest } from '../../components/islands/NetInterest'
import { budget, debt, vintageOf } from '../../data'

// Sections built so far. Everything else still falls through to the
// "Not built yet." placeholder below.
const built = new Set(['forty-trillion', 'structural-gap', 'what-congress-votes-on', 'net-interest'])

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="structural-gap">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 5</span></p>
    <h2>Revenue barely moves. Spending does.</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Revenue has sat in a narrow band for three decades. Outlays have not. The distance
      between the two lines is the deficit, in every single year.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Revenue averaged 17.2% of GDP across the 31 years. Outlays averaged 21.1%. A
      four-point gap, every year.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Revenue averaged 17.2% of GDP against outlays at 21.1% across FY1995-FY2025, a four-point gap that closed only in FY1998-FY2001."
      title="Federal revenue and outlays by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="revenue and outlays in nominal $ trillions, real $ trillions FY2025, or percent of GDP"
    >
      <StructuralGap client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      That gap shows up in years with no recession and no major tax bill, which is what
      makes it structural rather than circumstantial. The only four years it closed were
      1998 to 2001.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-congress-votes-on">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 6</span></p>
    <h2>The part they vote on is the part that shrank</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Discretionary spending is the only category set by an annual appropriation. It is
      also the only one smaller today, relative to GDP, than in 1995.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Share of GDP, FY1995 to FY2025: mandatory 9.8% to 13.7%, discretionary 7.2% to
      6.2%, net interest 3.1% to 3.2%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Share of GDP from FY1995 to FY2025: mandatory rose from 9.8% to 13.7%, discretionary fell from 7.2% to 6.2%, and net interest moved from 3.1% to 3.2%, dipping to 1.2% in FY2015 before nearly tripling."
      title="Mandatory, discretionary and net interest spending by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="mandatory, discretionary and net interest spending in nominal $ trillions, real $ trillions FY2025, or percent of GDP"
      note="Mandatory is net of offsetting receipts, the basis used throughout this section."
    >
      <VotedAndNot client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      In real terms mandatory spending — net of offsetting receipts, the basis used
      throughout this section — grew 194% over this span and discretionary grew 79%.
      Mandatory runs on permanent law and net interest is set by past borrowing; neither
      requires an annual vote.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Net interest looks flat endpoint to endpoint, 3.1% to 3.2%. It is not. It fell to
      1.2% of GDP by 2015 and has nearly tripled since. The endpoints hide the
      trajectory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="net-interest">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 7</span></p>
    <h2>Nobody appropriates the interest</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Net interest is the compounding cost of every prior year's borrowing. It passed $1
      trillion in FY2025 and now exceeds defence spending.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      $232 billion in FY1995, $970 billion in FY2025. Across the 31 years, $9.4
      trillion, which is 39% of all deficits.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Net interest rose from $232 billion in FY1995 to $970 billion in FY2025, totaling $9.4 trillion across the 31 years, 39% of all deficits."
      title="Net interest payments by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="net interest in nominal $ trillions, or real $ trillions FY2025"
    >
      <NetInterest client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Thirty-nine cents of every borrowed dollar went to servicing the borrowing that
      came before it. There is no vote on this, no appropriation, no debate.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The FY2015 figure of $223 billion is a recent trough, not the series low. The
      actual low is FY2003 at $153 billion. See <a href="#how-old">how old the debt is</a>
      for why this curve is so steep.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.filter((s) => !built.has(s.id)).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
